        "t1": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>Charlotte projects, neighborhood by neighborhood</strong></p><p>Charlotte's older neighborhoods and its lakes ask for different things. In Myers Park, Dilworth and Eastover, the usual project is a rear deck or a porch opening onto a pool. A glass deck railing there keeps the backyard in view. SouthPark and Ballantyne add newer two-story homes with upper balconies and open staircases that take a glass stair railing in place of wood balusters.</p><p>North of the city, Lake Norman is the big draw. Waterfront homes in Cornelius, Davidson, Huntersville and Mooresville have multi-level decks facing the water, and a frameless glass railing keeps the lake view clear. Lake Wylie, southwest on the Catawba River, is similar on a smaller scale. Across the state line, Fort Mill and Indian Land are full of new builds. Those addresses fall under South Carolina's 2021 code rather than the North Carolina code.</p>"
          }
        },
        "t2": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>Permits and the code in Mecklenburg County</strong></p><p>Charlotte and the Mecklenburg towns, including Cornelius, Davidson, Huntersville, Matthews and Mint Hill, get their building permits through Mecklenburg County Code Enforcement. Mooresville is in Iredell County. Lake Wylie addresses fall under Mecklenburg, Gaston or York County, South Carolina, depending on the shore. The 2018 North Carolina Residential and Building Codes apply. The North Carolina code sets guards at 36 inches minimum for houses and 42 inches for commercial and multifamily buildings.</p><p>For a pool deck, the residential code's Appendix G sets a 48-inch barrier, which our 46-inch panel in spigots gives you. Our engineering documents, with testing and blocking details for wood and concrete, are available for the permit file. Check with your local building department about whether your deck or railing needs a permit; a new deck usually does. Read the summary on our <a href=\"/pages/north-carolina-building-code-for-glass-railings\">North Carolina building code for glass railings</a> page before you finalize sizes.</p>"
          }
        },
        "t3": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>Charlotte's climate and a railing that keeps up</strong></p><p>Charlotte sits in the Piedmont, with humid summers around 90 degrees, mild winters and the occasional ice storm. Wood railings here need staining every couple of years and still gray out. Tempered 12 mm glass does not care about humidity. 2205 marine-grade stainless steel will not rust on a lakefront deck or a pool surround with chlorine splash. The hardware carries a 10-year no-rust promise, and the glass a lifetime warranty against manufacturing defects.</p><p>Pollen season in April leaves a yellow film on everything in Charlotte, glass included. It rinses off with a hose, which is easier than cleaning between pickets. Afternoon thunderstorms in July and August bring gusty wind; the spigots are bolted to the deck framing or slab, and the engineering documents show the blocking that holds them.</p>"
          }
        }
